{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.45,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["R49"],
      "pins": ["R49.1", "R49.2"],
      "nets": ["UART2_TX_FCC", "UART2_RX_NAVC"],
      "observed": "On the Connections sheet, the 'UART Connections' box has two rows. Row 1 is complete (UART2_RX_FCC -- R48=10 -- UART2_TX_NAVC). Row 2 is broken: R49 has an empty value field and its right-hand global-label box is blank, so the net UART2_TX_FCC enters R49 and stops there; nothing carries it to UART2_RX_NAVC, and the NAVC's UART2_RX pin is left with no driver from the FCC.",
      "expected": "The specification states the two MCUs are joined by UART2 ('NAVC↔FCC run UART2 + I2C + GPIO between the two MCUs'), and the FCC/NAVC notes both confirm UART2 as the inter-MCU link. R49 should therefore mirror R48 (series 10 Ω) and bridge UART2_TX_FCC to UART2_RX_NAVC so the FCC's transmit reaches the NAVC's receive.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Connections sheet, 'UART Connections' box: top row 'UART2_RX_FCC / R48 10 / UART2_TX_NAVC'; bottom row 'UART2_TX_FCC / R49 (value blank) / [label box blank]'"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "NAVC↔FCC run UART2 + I2C + GPIO between the two MCUs (confirmed in Hades.sch)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The FCC→NAVC direction of the UART2 inter-processor link is open; the navigation computer never receives data sent by the flight-control computer on UART2, breaking the intended full-duplex NAVC↔FCC communication.",
      "confidence": 0.45
    }
  ],
  "summary": "All explicitly specified electrical values (R1=10 mΩ shunt, L1=15 µH, 6k8/1k3 divider→5 V, LD39200 3V3, MIC26903, INA219 0x40, sensor addresses, crystals, SPDT boot switches) match the drawing, and the barometer RESET/EOC swap is a documented known bug. The one deviation the drawing supports is on the Connections sheet: the UART2 FCC→NAVC transmit path (R49) is left with a missing series value and a missing far-side net label, opening the inter-MCU UART2 link."
}

